{"name":"Pictorial Carmine","hex":"#c30b4e","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pictorial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, composed of, or illustrated by pictures.","Described or otherwise represented as if in a picture; graphic or vivid."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A newspaper or magazine with many pictures, or section thereof.","An article primarily featuring many photographs, or simply a collection of photographs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pictorial"},{"term":"carmine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A purplish-red pigment, made from dye obtained from the cochineal beetle; carminic acid or any of its derivatives.","A purplish-red colour, resembling that pigment."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the purplish red colour shade carmine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carmine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carmine","blocks":[{"text":"Carmine – also called cochineal, cochineal extract, crimson lake, or carmine lake – is a pigment of a bright-red color obtained from the aluminium complex derived from carminic acid. Specific code names for the pigment include natural red 4, C.I. 75470, or E120. Carmine is also a general term for a particularly deep-red color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Carmine.svg/330px-Carmine.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmine"},{"title":"Carmine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Carmine color is the general term for some deep red colors that are very slightly purplish but are generally slightly closer to pure red than the color crimson is. Some rubies have the color shown below as rich carmine. The deep dark red color shown at right as carmine is the color of the raw unprocessed pigment, but lighter, richer, or brighter colors are produced when the raw pigment is processed, some of which are shown below."}],"